Title: Decimal to Binary
Write a program to convert a Decimal number to a binary number using a stack.
Software Used: „C‟ programming tool (DEV C++)
Theory:
A stack is a linear data structure that follows the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle — meaning the
element that is inserted last is removed first.
When converting a decimal number to binary, we repeatedly divide the number by 2 and store the
remainders.
Since the first remainder corresponds to the least significant bit (LSB), we must reverse the order of
remainders to form the correct binary number.
This reversal can be easily done using a stack, because when we push all remainders and then pop
them, they come out in reverse order — giving the correct binary representation.
Syntax:
Push operation
stack[++top] = value;
Pop operation
value = stack[top--];
Algorithm:

Step 1: Start
Step 2: Declare an integer array stack[50] and set top = -1
Step 3: Read the decimal number num
Step 4: Repeat while num > 0
a. Find remainder rem = num % 2
b. Push remainder into stack → stack[++top] = rem
c. Divide num = num / 2
Step 5: Print “Binary number:”


Step 6: While top >= 0
a. Print stack[top]
b. Decrement top
Step 7: Exit
Terminate the program

Conclusion:



Program:
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
int stack[50], top = -1;
int num, rem;
printf("Enter a decimal number: ");
scanf("%d", &num);
// Convert decimal to binary and push remainders
while (num > 0) {
rem = num % 2;
stack[++top] = rem;
num = num / 2;
}
printf("Binary number: ");
// Pop and print elements from stack
while (top >= 0) {
printf("%d", stack[top--]);
}
return 0;
}
Output:
Enter a decimal number: 25
Binary number: 11001
